Sculpt and rig the **Sunfront Sunhawk**, a wide, flat gunship aircraft whose two
rotors spin while its underslung forward cannon aims up and down — as a 64x36x64
opaque-voxel model using only the `voxel-anim` tool. You build it by **painting
discrete opaque cells** one recorded operation at a time, and you **author its rotor,
hover, and gun-run animations** as F-curves.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief tells
you *what the Sunhawk is* and *how it must move*, but the parts you build, how you break
the aircraft into moving pieces, where you place the joints, and how you shape every
form are **entirely yours to invent**. Work out the pieces a hovering, firing gunship
needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already on
your `PATH`. It is the sole channel: you build the model by calling it **one operation
at a time**, and the ordered operations you issue — recorded per part to
`parts/<part>.actions.json` — together with the rig you define are the **authoritative
output that is scored**, not the voxels on disk. Anything produced another way (writing
files directly, editing a preview by hand) is discarded and flagged; only the recorded
operations and the rig count.

Every voxel operation targets a **part** with `--part <name>`; each part is sculpted
**separately** in its own preview and log. The volume starts **empty**. Voxels are
**opaque** `#rrggbb` colors — there is no transparency. After each call the binary
re-renders that part's isometric preview to `parts/<part>.png` and appends the
operation to that part's log.

**You invent the rig.** Define whatever parts you need with `define-part` (giving each
a parent and a pivot), place joints on them with `define-joint`, and author the
required animations with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Every voxel operation
targets a **part** with `--part <name>`, so define a part before you sculpt into it;
each part is sculpted separately in the shared volume's coordinates.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
voxel-anim define-part --name hull
voxel-anim fill-box --part hull --x0 6 --y0 6 --z0 8 --x1 57 --y1 22 --z1 57 --color "#c69a4b"
```

(The part name above is just an example — name your own parts however you like.) Run
`voxel-anim --help` to list every operation **and** the rig subcommands (defining
parts, setting pivots, defining joints, and authoring animations with
`define-animation`/`add-keyframe`), and `voxel-anim <operation> --help` for one
operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. **Read
`parts/<part>.png` after your calls** to see that part's progress, **and read the
assembled-scene previews under `scene/` (`scene/iso.png`, `scene/front.png`,
`scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** — the whole aircraft composed from all your parts,
re-rendered after every operation. The scene views are how you confirm the parts
actually fit together: that the rotors sit out on their side mounts and the cannon
hangs under the nose. Because each part is sculpted in the shared volume's coordinates,
position each part where it belongs on the finished aircraft; the scene simply composes
them there.

